  "marketSentiment": "NEUTRAL_BEARISH",
  "macroTheme": "Fed delivered first hike since 2023 (+25bp to 3.75-4.00%) with hawkish Warsh signalling a campaign, not one-and-done. 16 of 18 officials project at least one more hike by year-end; CME FedWatch prices 55.4% probability of October follow-up. 10Y yield sustained above 5% for first time since 2007. USD surged 1.1% — biggest weekly gain in 3+ months. Dow fell for 3rd straight week while Nasdaq rallied on clarity trade. Gold and silver rallied Friday despite hawkish backdrop — the most counterintuitive move of the week.",
  "macroOverview": "The week was defined by the FOMC's first rate hike since 2023 and Chair Warsh's unequivocally hawkish press conference. The dot plot raised the median 2026 terminal rate to 4.1% with 16 of 18 officials projecting further tightening. The 10Y Treasury yield pierced 5.04% intraweek before settling at 5.006%, forcing a fundamental repricing of equity risk premiums. The USD surged 1.1% — its strongest week in more than three months. Oil remained elevated with Brent spiking above $110 on Houthi advances toward the Bab el-Mandeb before fading to $104. The Dow fell for a third consecutive week while the NAS100 rallied +0.94% as tech investors embraced 'clarity over level'. Gold and silver staged the most confounding rally: both rose Friday despite the overwhelmingly hawkish rates backdrop, suggesting either a short-squeeze or a reawakening Middle East safe-haven bid. The BoE held at 3.75% (7-2 hawkish dissent), the BoJ held at 0.50%. The week ahead carries no inflation prints — the data vacuum leaves the 5% yield and central bank momentum as the sole steering currents.",
